tems begin to work together a  gripping science fiction Thriller  Colossus the forbin project dispenses  with action and adventure instead opting  for a slow deliberately paced narrative  set mostly in boardrooms and control  rooms what the movie lacks in action  however it more than makes up for in  suspense in the late 1960s an aspiring  young director named George Lucas  crafted a 15-minute science fiction  short about a totalitarian state that  Demands a total lack of emotion from its  citizens it was titled electronic  Labyrinth  thx-1138 4eb the movie became the basis  for one of the first projects  distributed by Warner Brothers under the  American zoetrope Banner a production  house started by Francis Ford Coppola to  nurture creative young talent and  independent filmmaking the full-length  feature  thx-1138 was Lucas's feature film debut  thx1138 is set in an underground World  devoid of Love passion hate or anger as  Free Will and thought are squashed by  the state when one man and one woman  stopped taking the medication that  suppresses feelings however they are  confronted by the true horror of their  world and fall in love in the process  they experience emotions for the first  time and immediately become fugitives  from the law a glimpse into a harrowing  future  thx-1138 gained more attention after the  release of Lucas's Star Wars and has  since become a revered classic for its  exploration of the nature of free will  you do  for a living I mean oh just visiting  many cult classics can stake a claim  among the best sci-fi films of the 1970s  but none are quite as unusual and  offbeat as the man who fell to Earth  most notable perhaps for its lead  performance by David Bowie the film was  barely given a theatrical release  reportedly because Paramount Executives  were so perplexed by it over the years  however the man who fell to Earth has  garnered a passionate fan following not  just for Bowie's charming and affecting  performance but also its Eerie tone and  compelling story Bowie stars as an alien  who hails from a world on which an  environmental crisis has left the planet  dry and Barren coming to Earth seeking  water he takes on a human identity and  sets himself up as the CEO of a major  technology firm in order to save his  Homeworld soon though he becomes a  wealthy Tycoon and slowly Falls prey to  Earth's many Temptations including the  love of a beautiful woman today the man  who fell to Earth is considered a bona  fide sci-fi Gem and its influence lives  on in a surprising limited series remake  that was launched in 2022  it's hard to remake a classic movie and  do it justice but the 1978 Invasion of  the Body Snatchers did just that and  then some retelling the same story as  the 1956 film of the same name sometimes  cited as one of the best remakes ever  the film brought the story of a Sinister  Alien Invasion into the 1970s mixing it  with the tone of the decade's best  political Thrillers and loading it with  relevant social commentary in this  frightening reboot of the original movie  we meet San francisco-based scientific  researcher Elizabeth Driscoll who is  Disturbed when her boyfriend begins  acting strangely it soon becomes clear  that a strange Alien Force is creating  duplicates of human beings forcing  Driscoll and her allies to flee from a  city of doppelgangers a stunning  suspense-filled Alien Invasion story  with few equals Invasion of the Body  Snatchers also happens to boast one of  the bleakest endings in sci-fi movie  history the sub-genre of  post-apocalyptic sci-fi gained steam in  the 1970s and it arguably reached its  Zenith with the ultra low budget  Australian film Mad Max George Miller's  ability to carefully craft a high  quality futuristic action movie on a  shoestring budget shook the filmmaking  world with a cottage industry of  wannabes springing up in its wake  in Mad Max most of society has all but  Fallen apart due to global oil shortages  as survivors try to eke out a meager  living local Warlords and gangs of  Ruthless bikers attempt to take  advantage of the chaos but one of the  last remnants of organized law  enforcement a cop named Max rocketansky  is out to stop them still one of the  best movies of a genre Mad Max was a  sensation in its native Australia thanks  to its gear grinding action and  over-the-top violence  it found just as much success Stateside  turning Mel Gibson into a star and  kick-starting George Miller's  directorial career eventually followed  by a pair of sequels Mad Max was later  rebooted in 2015 with Mad Max Fury Road  a critically acclaimed Blockbuster that  reignited the franchise for the 21st  century  oh what a day  The Man Behind Some of the best movies  of all time Stanley Kubrick also  directed one of the greatest dystopian  sci-fi Classics A Clockwork Orange based  on the celebrated 1962 novel by Anthony  Burgess the movie stars Malcolm McDowell  and a broad ensemble cast of  lesser-known actors in a story about a  dismal future that mixes unsettling  surrealism with sharp social commentary  a film that often borders on the  avant-garde A Clockwork Orange revolves  around Alex delarge in eccentric Street  Punk who leads in Ultra violent gang of  thugs as they steal assault and murder  innocent people eventually Alex is  apprehended by the authorities who force  him to undergo a brutal form of  brainwashing a film that cinematically  breathtaking and disturbing in equal  measure A Clockwork Orange is today  considered to be one of the truly iconic  films of the 1970s young director Steven  Spielberg had done plenty of work for  television in the early 1970s and his  transition to feature films yielded  results almost immediately in his second  film Jaws he produced one of the biggest  hits of the decade in popularized the  term Blockbuster to follow it up he  turned up Science Fiction with a  poignant story that mixed family drama  and an outer space mystery the 1977  classic Close Encounters of the Third  Kind while Star Wars blue audiences away  with its fast-moving in-space Opera  action Spielberg went a different route  opting for an emotional drama told  through a story of alien visitation here  Family Man Roy Neri is contacted by an  extraterrestrial intelligence and  becomes a conduit for communication when  they arrive on Earth niri's personal  Journey sits at the heart of the film as  he struggles to understand what is  happening while his family life  deteriorates around him a visually  captivating experience that fuses  mystery Terror wonder and awe Close  Encounters of the Third Kind was  nominated for nine Academy Awards  including best director for Spielberg  while it may not have been the biggest  sci-fi film at the box office in 1977 it  was probably the most moving based on a  novel by polish author stanislaw lamb  The Landmark film Solaris might be  Russia's greatest contribution to the  Sci-Fi genre while many American  audiences still haven't given the film  its proper due critics and sci-fi Buffs  have rightly hailed it as a milestone  released in 1972 the film centers on  Chris Kelvin a psychologist who is sent  to a space station orbiting the planet  Solaris on a mission to assess the  mental condition of its crew following a  series of unexplainable emotional  breakdowns Chris soon begins to suffer  himself due to an unknown phenomenon  that has struck the station as he learns  more about the aliens and Solaris itself  Klein's experience also helps him deal  with the trauma that has haunted him for  years a spellbinding psychological drama  Solaris has always been highly acclaimed  but long underrated and underseen by  wider audiences that perhaps helps  explain why director Stephen Soderberg  and star George Clooney remade the film  in 2002 in an effort to shine a light on  an underappreciated classic you've taken  your first step into a larger word  George Lucas's sci-fi phenomenon Star  Wars was enough of a cultural Touchstone  that it deserved to be counted among the  best sci-fi films of the decade for its  Legacy alone Star Wars debuted to  massive crowds and lines that wrapped  around city blocks while children  skipped school and adults took off work  to go see it boasting groundbreaking  special effects the movie is a modern  day fairy tale about a young group of  Heroes Who band together to defeat an  evil Galactic Empire with its  unprecedented ticket sales and  mountainous merchandising Blitz Star  Wars changed the landscape of movies  forever every Studio wanted a piece of  the pie and while sequels were nothing  new in Tinseltown the era of Blockbuster  franchises and toy tie-ins truly began  in 1977 and it wasn't just the special  effects the lunch boxes and the box  office bucks either Star Wars was also a  well-crafted story with a heartfelt  emotional core despite being a  kid-friendly pulp comic book Space story  Allah Gordon critics were bowled over  showering the film with gushing praise  noted critic Roger Ebert who wasn't  typically impressed by flimsy  Blockbuster Fair happily awarded it four  stars and remarked on its awe-inspiring  nature no matter how you look at it when  it comes to Great 70 these sci-fi  Adventures Star Wars is unquestionably  King the 1970s were filled with great  science fiction movies that ran the  gamut from cerebral thought-provoking  message films to Breezy Space Adventures  but in 1979 an iconic film from  sophomore director Ridley Scott did  something truly staggering a grounded  realistic outer space Chiller that was  unlike anything audiences had  experienced before alien delivered a  story that expertly Blended horror and  science fiction screenwriter Dan  O'Bannon created a world that mixed the  lived-in grittiness of Star Wars with a  cast of ordinary men and women rather  than Larger than Life Heroes and  colorful creatures  these were not Intrepid space  adventurers but blue collar workers  long-haul space truckers dealing